WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.290 align:start position:0%
 
foreign  getaway plans grounded again  after months of travel chaos and  cancellations British Airways has  suspended the sale of domestic and  European flights from Heathrow and now  Sky News understands it won't sell any  new short haul tickets until the 15th of  August  the airline says it's in response to a  cap on passenger numbers introduced by  Heathrow Airport which forced the  cancellation of thousands of flights the  suspension of ticket sales will allow it  to rebook those passengers affected more  easily  after huge disruption passenger numbers  at Heathrow are almost back up to  pre-pandemic levels the problem is the  number of ground staff working for  Airlines and baggage handling companies  hasn't recovered as well and is still  off by about 30 percent this is a big  problem for people who are hoping to  book in the next week but if you're  already booked on with a ba flight this  should give you a little bit more  confidence that if there's a  cancellation you have some room for  maneuver and as we're seeing more  airports introduce their passenger caps  and Airlines may have to follow in Ba's  footsteps and introduce this really  creative measure to try and give  themselves some predictability  this is the latest attempt by the  industry to get to grips with a crisis  caused by a lack of staff and more  passengers than Airlines and airports  can handle we've also had an Amnesty on  changing flight schedules and  cancellations caps on passenger numbers  at Heathrow Airport but each measure  creates knock-on effects more passengers  to be rebooked or compensated and that's  how we've got to this stage the risk is  this ends up hurting other Travelers as  it increases demand and prices for  competitors Services airfares will  Skyrocket now as a result of this  because there's less capacity for seats  to be available so this will force  airfares up and also because this is a  very short notice and the Outlook is  very uncertain there could be further  disruption  Heathrow has been heavily criticized by  some of its Airlines for the passenger  cap but it's not the only one  skipol airport in Amsterdam has also  limited passenger numbers and will  continue to till the end of October  but none of these measures will address  the underlying problem a lack of Staff  until that's sorted the risk of  disruption will continue through the  Autumn Madeleine Ratcliffe Sky News

